  "next_plan": {
    "families": [
      {
        "budget_share": 0.6,
        "family": "pipelined",
        "ranges": [
          {"choices": ["round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": -1, "param": "angle_guard"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 22, "low": 14, "param": "data_width"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "n_iter"}
        ]
      ,
        "why": "1 result/cycle -> throughput = Fmax, the only structurally reachable way to satisfy >= 50 MSPS"
      },
      {
        "budget_share": 0.4,
        "family": "pipelined_m",
        "ranges": [
          {"choices": ["round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": -1, "param": "angle_guard"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 22, "low": 14, "param": "data_width"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "n_iter"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 6, "low": 2, "param": "m"}
        ],
        "why": "same 1 result/cycle throughput but shorter logic depth between registers -> higher Fmax at lower FF cost; m trades latency for clock speed"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "Throughput is the binding constraint, not accuracy. iterative/unrolled_k are structurally capped: they emit 1 result every N+3 (resp. ceil(N/k)+3) cycles, and the observed best is 9.43 MSPS (iterative) and 8.14 MSPS (unrolled_k) against a 50 MSPS floor - a ~5x structural gap, not a tuning gap. k=8 would need ceil(N/8)+3 ~= 5-6 cycles/result, still short of 50 MSPS unless Fmax ~ 250-300 MHz, which the shared barrel-shifter datapath will not reach. pipelined/pipelined_m emit 1 result/cycle, so throughput ~= Fmax and 50 MSPS becomes reachable. Accuracy is already demonstrably achievable: data_width=17, n_iter=15-17 with angle_guard 0-3 / frac_guard 0-2 gives 2^-12.00 .. 2^-14.09, so 12 bits costs ~17-bit datapath with modest n_iter. data_width is opened to 14..22 to also expose the low-power end of the power/accuracy front (quantisation floor ~ W-2 bits) so the hypervolume can cover both axes."
  }
  ,
  "rationale": "0/60 feasible, and 100% of evaluations violate throughput while only 53% violate accuracy - the failure mode is entirely throughput. Both explored families are inherently multi-cycle (N+3 and ceil(N/k)+3 cycles/result); their observed ceilings (9.43 and 8.14 MSPS) are ~5x below the 50 MSPS floor, so refining ranges inside them cannot produce a feasible point. The error target is comfortably reachable at data_width=17 (best 2^-14.09, and 2^-12.00 already at n_iter=16), so the missing piece is a 1-result/cycle architecture: pipelined and pipelined_m. Rounds 2-3 should evaluate those, then a final map_front round can fill the power/accuracy curve."
}

{
  "decision": "infeasible",
  "rationale": "The binding constraint is throughput_msps >= 50, and it is unreachable for every design evaluated. 100% of the 100 evaluations violate it; the best throughput seen anywhere is 13.6 MSPS (iterative), and the best unrolled_k reaches only 8.14 MSPS — a >3.6x shortfall against the 50 MSPS floor. The accuracy constraint is clearly satisfiable (best max_abs_err 1.35e-07 = 2^-22.83, well inside 2^-12), so accuracy is not the blocker; the multi-cycle schedule of the explored families (iterative: N+3 cycles/result; unrolled_k: ceil(N/k)+3 cycles/result) caps throughput far below 50 MSPS even at the highest Fmax observed. With the summary reporting no families left unexplored and 0 of 100 designs feasible, no registry design can simultaneously satisfy throughput >= 50 MSPS and max_abs_err <= 2^-12, so the spec is infeasible rather than merely under-mapped."
  ,"next_plan": null
}
